    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d29">

                <pb facs="0083" n="51" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v06/pg51"/>

                <head>29. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in Cuba<anchor n="1" xml:id="d29-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1958-03-05T18:38:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 5,
                                1958—6:38 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>470. Embtel 490.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d29-f2"
                    /> Department pleased learn steps contemplated GOC create favorable election climate (Embtel 500<anchor n="3" xml:id="d29-f3"/>) as suggested points (A)
                    and (C) second paragraph Embtel, and feels
                    you should express these sentiments to Batista. At same time you may, without using pressure, and if
                    you feel your remarks will not be regarded as intervention, suggest steps (D),
                    (E) with some modification, and (F).</p>
                <p>Department feels step (E) should be enlarged in scope to include leaders Church,
                    labor unions, press and political opposition, etc., who would meet with
                        Batista to discuss ways and means
                    assuring their respective organizations honest elections will be held.</p>
                <p>Timing of approach to Batista left your
                    discretion.</p>
                <pb facs="0084" n="52" xml:id="pg52"/>
                <p>With further regard point (C), please note discussion this point in separate
                    message being sent reporting Rubottom’s
                    testimony before Senate Committee today.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d29-f4"/> He did
                    not oppose Cuba’s requesting UN observers but did
                    consider inappropriate for US govt to make such proposal to UN.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
